  ✗ REJECTED: Liquidation allows collateral extraction beyond the borrower
    Reason: Upon reviewing the liquidation and _repay functions in 
LybraEUSDVaultBase.sol, I determined that the alleged oversized-repayment 
mismatch is not exploitable through the shown liquidation path. Although _repay 
caps the repayment amount to borrowed, liquidation requires the borrower’s 
collateral ratio to be below 150% and restricts assetAmount to at most 50% of 
depositedAsset. Given the ratio check, collateral value is less than 1.5 times 
the borrower’s debt; therefore, the requested liquidation value, assetAmount * 
assetPrice, is less than 75% of the debt and cannot exceed borrowed. 
Consequently, _repay does not silently reduce a larger requested repayment in 
any eligible liquidation, and reducedAsset (110% of assetAmount) is not based on
an over-large assetAmount relative to debt. The allowance check is weak because 
it only requires a positive allowance, but EUSD.burn still must burn the capped 
amount and does not create the hypothesized excess-collateral condition. The 
zero-debt case also cannot pass the ratio calculation.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Deployer retains direct DAO authority over critical configur
    Justification: Upon reviewing the GovernanceTimelock constructor and 
Configurator access-control modifiers, I confirmed that the hypothesized 
privilege bypass exists, and the implementation contains an even broader flaw. 
GovernanceTimelock.sol grants DAO to both address(this) and msg.sender via 
_grantRole(DAO, ...), so the deployer receives DAO authority without any 
timelock delay. Configurator's onlyRole(DAO) modifier calls checkOnlyRole(DAO, 
msg.sender), which merely returns hasRole(role, _sender) and does not revert 
when false; the returned boolean is ignored. Consequently, initToken, 
setMintVault, setMintVaultMaxSupply, and setBadCollateralRatio are callable by 
any address, including the deployer directly, without governance execution or 
delay. The same issue affects the checkRole modifier and all 
TIMELOCK/ADMIN-protected functions because GovernanceTimelock.checkRole also 
returns a boolean without enforcing it, while Configurator ignores that result. 
Therefore, no effective guard prevents direct calls, and the deployer-specific 
hypothesis is confirmed, although the actual vulnerability is not limited to the
deployment account and permits arbitrary callers to modify protected 
configuration.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Protocol reward distributions are permanently stranded when 
    Justification: Upon reviewing Configurator.distributeRewards and 
ProtocolRewardsPool.notifyRewardAmount, I confirmed the accounting vulnerability
exists. distributeRewards transfers the thresholded peUSD or eUSD balance to the
rewards pool before invoking notifyRewardAmount, without checking whether 
esLBR.totalSupply() is nonzero and without validating that notification 
succeeded. In notifyRewardAmount, the require(msg.sender == 
address(configurator)) check passes, but the function immediately returns when 
totalStaked() == 0, leaving rewardPerTokenStored and any pending-reward 
accounting unchanged. Because the transfer has already occurred, the tokens 
remain in ProtocolRewardsPool but are not assigned to current or future stakers 
through the reward-per-token mechanism. distributeRewards is externally 
callable, so an actor can invoke it whenever the configurator holds a threshold 
balance while no esLBR is staked; any protocol path that invokes it has the same
effect. The rewards pool has no visible recovery or pending-distribution 
mechanism, and later notifications account only for newly notified amounts. 
Thus, the funds are effectively stranded from the intended distribution 
accounting, although they remain held by the pool.
  ? UNCERTAIN: sendAndCall attributes transferred funds to the approved cal
    Note: Upon reviewing OFTCoreV2._sendAndCall and the PeUSD implementations of
_debitFrom, I confirmed an identity mismatch: _debitFrom(_from, ...) can debit 
an owner using the caller’s allowance, while _sendAndCall encodes msg.sender—not
_from—into the callback payload at OFTCoreV2.sol around lines 115-121. 
Consequently, an approved operator can initiate a send-and-call that burns the 
owner’s tokens but reports the operator as the callback source. The allowance 
checks in PeUSD.sol and PeUSDMainnetStableVision.sol only authorize the debit 
and do not reconcile the encoded identity. However, the provided source does not
include the destination IOFTReceiverV2 implementations or any remote callback 
consumers. The callback merely forwards the encoded value through 
callOnOFTReceived; exploitation requires a receiver to interpret that value as 
the token owner and grant an owner-dependent benefit. Therefore, the code 
clearly establishes a potentially dangerous semantic mismatch, but the existence
of a concrete exploitable authorization impact cannot be confirmed without 
reviewing the destination receiver contracts and their use of _from.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Boosted rewards can be applied retroactively
    Justification: Upon reviewing the updateReward modifier, getBoost, earned, 
and esLBRBoost.getUserBoost implementations, I confirmed that the accounting 
error is exploitable. updateReward records only one userUpdatedAt timestamp and 
earned() computes the entire uncheckpointed reward-per-token delta using the 
boost returned at claim or another update time. After a user stakes, the user 
can call esLBRBoost.setLockStatus near the end of the accrual period without any
corresponding checkpoint in StakingRewardsV2. getBoost then passes the old 
userUpdatedAt to getUserBoost, which observes the newly created lock. While the 
lock remains active, getUserBoost returns the full miningBoost, causing earned()
to multiply rewards accrued before the lock by the newly obtained boost. Calling
getReward, stake, or withdraw then permanently checkpoints this inflated amount.
There is no callback, access-control relationship, or synchronization mechanism 
forcing staking rewards to be checkpointed before setLockStatus, and the boost 
contract does not retain historical lock states. Therefore, the implementation 
permits retroactive application of a boost to previously accrued rewards.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Reward duration accepts zero and reward scheduling truncates
    Justification: Upon reviewing the StakingRewardsV2 contract, I confirmed the
reported issue in setRewardsDuration and notifyRewardAmount. setRewardsDuration 
accepts any uint256 value, including zero, and only checks that the prior reward
period has ended; it does not enforce a positive or minimum duration. Once the 
owner sets duration to zero, a later notifyRewardAmount call reaches either 
_amount / duration or (_amount + remainingRewards) / duration, causing a 
Solidity division-by-zero panic before the reward-ratio validation and 
preventing new rewards from being scheduled. The onlyOwner modifier limits this 
to the owner or compromised/misconfigured governance, but it does not eliminate 
the operational denial-of-service risk. Additionally, both reward-rate 
calculations use integer division, so amounts that are not evenly divisible by 
duration are truncated. The resulting rewardRatio multiplied by duration can be 
less than the scheduled amount, with no remainder tracking or refund mechanism; 
sufficiently small amounts can produce rewardRatio == 0 and revert. Therefore, 
the hypothesis is supported by the implementation.
  ✗ REJECTED: Stablecoin reward accounting uses token decimals as a diviso
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged decimal-conversion vulnerability in 
ProtocolRewardsPool.notifyRewardAmount and getReward, I determined this is a 
false positive. For tokenType == 1, notifyRewardAmount computes 
rewardPerTokenStored using (amount * 1e36 / token.decimals()) / totalStaked(). 
Although token.decimals() is numerically used as a divisor, the preceding 1e36 
factor makes this mathematically equivalent to converting the raw token amount 
into 18-decimal accounting units (amount * 1e18 / 10**decimals) and then 
applying the standard 1e18 reward-per-token scale. In getReward, the inverse 
conversion is explicitly performed as (reward - eUSDShare - peUSDBalance) * 
token.decimals() / 1e18. For a 6-decimal token, the accounting factor is 
therefore 1e36 / 6 in the first expression and multiplied by 6 / 1e18 during 
redemption, with the numeric decimals factor canceling consistently across the 
system. Consequently, distributing a token through 
Configurator.distributeRewards does not inherently inflate rewards as alleged. 
The implementation may be less readable than using powers of ten, but the 
claimed exploitable accounting error is not present.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: LBR oracle result is used without zero, sign, or freshness v
    Justification: Upon reviewing the oracle-dependent functions in 
EUSDMiningIncentives.sol, I confirmed the reported oracle-validation weakness is
exploitable. In purchaseOtherEarnings, the LBR feed result from 
latestRoundData() is destructured while roundId, updatedAt, answeredInRound, and
validity of the signed answer are ignored. The code directly converts lbrPrice 
to uint256 and calculates the EUSD bidding fee without checking that the answer 
is positive, nonzero, complete, or fresh. A zero answer therefore reduces the 
fee to zero, allowing an otherwise eligible user to receive esLBR rewards 
without paying the intended EUSD cost. Negative answers can produce an invalid 
large unsigned value and commonly cause arithmetic overflow or transfer failure,
creating a denial-of-service or reverted buyout path. Additionally, 
stakedLBRLpValue, used by isOtherEarningsClaimable, independently consumes the 
same unvalidated LBR oracle result, so zero or malformed data can also affect 
eligibility or revert. The isEUSDBuyoutAllowed flag only enables or disables the
entire payment mode and does not validate oracle data.
  ✗ REJECTED: Exact-role authorization can block governance administrators
    Reason: Upon reviewing the authorization logic in GovernanceTimelock, 
LybraGovernance._execute, and LybraConfigurator, I determined that the alleged 
vulnerability is not demonstrated as an exploitable authorization or 
availability flaw. GovernanceTimelock.checkOnlyRole intentionally requires the 
specified operational role, while checkRole explicitly implements the intended 
hierarchy by allowing either the requested role or DAO. Consequently, 
Configurator functions protected by checkRole(TIMELOCK) or checkRole(ADMIN) 
remain callable by the DAO through the timelock, which is granted DAO in the 
GovernanceTimelock constructor. DAO-only Configurator functions similarly 
correctly require DAO. Although GOV is not automatically treated as an 
operational role, GOV is configured as the admin of DAO, TIMELOCK, and ADMIN, 
allowing a GOV holder to grant the necessary operational role through inherited 
AccessControl role-management functions. The LybraGovernance._execute 
checkOnlyRole(TIMELOCK) is restrictive by design and requires the deployment to 
assign TIMELOCK to the intended executor; the constructor's omission is a 
configuration concern, not proof that GOV administrators must perform that 
operation directly. The hypothesis therefore conflates role administration with 
operational authorization.
  ? UNCERTAIN: PeUSD collateral valuation may accept stale or invalid oracl
    Note: Upon reviewing LybraPeUSDVaultBase and its derived vaults, I found 
that getAssetPrice() directly invokes etherOracle.fetchPrice() in _etherPrice() 
without checking that the returned value is nonzero, positive, fresh, complete, 
or otherwise valid. The result is used by _mintPeUSD(), _checkHealth(), 
withdraw(), liquidation(), rigidRedemption(), and the derived LybraRETHVault and
LybraWstETHVault price calculations. Consequently, the vault layer would accept 
an inflated or stale value if the configured oracle returns one, and no local 
guard would prevent the resulting over-minting or unsafe withdrawal. However, 
the supplied source does not include the implementation of the external oracle, 
and the oracle address is immutable per vault constructor rather than 
dynamically selected by Configurator. The comments identify it as a 
Liquity-style PriceFeed, which may itself enforce freshness and validity. 
Therefore, the absence of validation in this layer is a real defense-in-depth 
weakness, but exploitability and severity cannot be conclusively established 
without reviewing the concrete fetchPrice() implementation and its 
update/fallback behavior.
  ✗ REJECTED: Governance proposals can pass without an affirmative majorit
    Reason: Upon reviewing the proposal evaluation logic in LybraGovernance, I 
determined that the alleged affirmative-majority vulnerability is a false 
positive. The overridden _quorumReached function requires supportVotes[1] plus 
supportVotes[2] to reach one-third of the snapshot supply, while _voteSucceeded 
independently requires supportVotes[1] to be strictly greater than 
supportVotes[0]. Under OpenZeppelin Governor Bravo counting conventions, support
value 0 is Against, 1 is For, and 2 is Abstain; therefore, the success predicate
does enforce a strict affirmative majority and does not allow quorum alone to 
pass a proposal. The proposals() view function labels these indexes 
inconsistently, reporting index 0 as forVotes and index 1 as againstVotes, which
may have caused the hypothesis, but this presentation does not alter the 
underlying decision logic. A zero-for or tied vote fails _voteSucceeded, and a 
minority-for vote also fails even if quorum is reached. The separate _execute 
override hardcodes proposal ID 1, but that is outside the reviewed hypothesis.
  ? UNCERTAIN: Proxy upgrades are controlled outside the timelocked governa
    Note: After analyzing LybraProxy, GovernanceTimelock, and Configurator, I 
cannot definitively confirm or reject the alleged access-control vulnerability 
from the supplied code. LybraProxy simply inherits TransparentUpgradeableProxy 
and passes the arbitrary constructor parameter _admin to the OpenZeppelin proxy 
implementation; no check requires that _admin be GovernanceTimelock or a 
timelock-controlled ProxyAdmin. Likewise, GovernanceTimelock defines 
role-management and checkRole/checkOnlyRole functions, but neither it nor 
Configurator contains any proxy-upgrade authorization or binding to the proxy 
administrator. This demonstrates that governance linkage is not enforced by 
these contracts. However, the alleged exploit depends on the omitted 
LybraProxyAdmin implementation and deployment configuration: the administrator 
could already be owned by GovernanceTimelock, a multisignature, or another 
controlled mechanism. The source does not show the admin owner, upgrade 
functions, initialization, or deployed addresses, so compromise of an 
independent privileged account cannot be established. Reviewing LybraProxyAdmin 
and deployment scripts or on-chain ownership and role assignments is required 
for a definitive verdict.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: StETH rebasing can make recorded collateral exceed the vault
    Justification: Upon reviewing LybraStETHDepositVault and LybraEUSDVaultBase,
I confirmed the accounting mismatch is present and can become exploitable after 
an adverse stETH rebase. In depositEtherToMint, the vault records msg.value in 
both totalDepositedAsset and depositedAsset, even though Lido returns 
sharesAmount and the vault’s actual stETH balance is subject to rebasing. 
Similarly, depositAssetToMint records the nominal token amount rather than a 
share-based entitlement. The collateral-ratio checks in _checkHealth, 
liquidation, superLiquidation, rigidRedemption, and _withdraw all use 
depositedAsset, while getAssetPrice returns only the ETH oracle price in the 
stETH vault. Consequently, a negative rebase reduces 
collateralAsset.balanceOf(address(this)) without reducing recorded collateral or
debt capacity. Withdrawals and liquidation may then attempt to transfer more 
stETH than the vault possesses and revert, leaving undercollateralized debt 
effectively unrecoverable. The positive-rebase excessIncomeDistribution path 
does not correct negative rebases. This is therefore a genuine accounting and 
insolvency risk, although exploitation depends on an adverse rebase occurring.
  ✗ REJECTED: Cross-chain OFT amount encoding can truncate or reject overs
    Reason: Upon reviewing OFTCoreV2._send, _sendAndCall, and _ld2sd, I 
determined this hypothesis is a false positive for value truncation or permanent
loss. The implementation removes dust, invokes _debitFrom, and then encodes the 
shared-unit amount through _ld2sd. Although _ld2sd converts to uint64, it 
explicitly requires amountSD <= type(uint64).max before performing the cast; 
oversized amounts therefore revert rather than truncate. In addition, the check 
occurs in the same transaction after _debitFrom, so any burn performed by 
PeUSD._debitFrom or PeUSDMainnet._debitFrom is atomically reverted when the 
overflow requirement fails. The same applies to send-and-call flows. Amounts at 
or below the maximum are safely encoded, while amounts above the limit fail 
before LayerZero submission. An application can intentionally submit an 
oversized transfer and incur a failed transaction, but the reviewed code 
provides no mechanism to force another user’s debit or create permanent 
accounting divergence. Accordingly, the alleged loss and truncation path is 
mitigated by the explicit checked conversion and EVM transaction atomicity.


